== Exercise 5.1: seven definitions of a tree
OK   5.1 the seven definitions agree on every graph with 1..6 vertices  [33867 graphs, 1442 trees]
OK   5.1 Lemma A: an edge uv is on a cycle iff u, v stay connected in G - uv
OK   5.1 Lemma B: E >= V - c, with equality iff acyclic
OK   5.1 tree counts 1, 1, 3, 16, 125, 1296 (Cayley n^(n-2))
== Exercise 5.2: two vertices of degree 1
OK   5.2 every connected acyclic graph with n >= 2 has >= 2 vertices of degree 1  [3241 trees]
OK   5.2 both ends of every longest path have degree 1
== Exercise 5.3: bipartite graphs
OK   5.3(a) every tree is bipartite
OK   5.3(b) bipartite <=> every cycle is even  [2599 graphs]
OK   5.3(c) WFS depth parity + edge test == brute force (stack, queue, random bag)
== Exercise 5.4: pecking orders (tournaments)
OK   5.4(a,b) the insertion rule always yields a procession  [1899 small + 150 large tournaments]
OK   5.4(c) a tournament with a directed cycle has a 3-cycle
OK   5.4(c) no 3-cycle (n >= 3) => exactly one procession
OK   5.4(c) ... and each pigeon's position is 1 + the number of pigeons it pecks
== Exercise 5.5: Euler tours
OK   5.5(a,b) connected graph: Euler tour exists (brute force) <=> all degrees even  [1099 graphs]
OK   5.5(c) Walk/Hierholzer returns a valid tour exactly when one exists
OK   5.5(c) parity: loops resume only at even moments; an edge taken at an even moment makes the caller's vertex the first entry appended inside the call  [1414 random Eulerian graphs]
OK   5.5(c) parity: the condition matters — on the triangle, Walk(b) called from Walk(a) appends s first
== Exercise 5.6: hypercubes
OK   5.6(a) reflected Gray code is a Hamiltonian cycle of Q_d, d = 2..12
OK   5.6(b) Q_d has an Euler tour iff d is even (d = 0..8; brute force for d <= 3)
== Exercise 5.7: strongly connected orientations
OK   5.7 strong orientation exists (all 2^E tried) <=> connected and every edge on a cycle  [1378 graphs]
OK   5.7 (*) RecursiveDFS: every edge joins an ancestor and a descendant
OK   5.7 the DFS orientation is strongly connected exactly when some orientation is
== Exercise 5.8: a tree that is both depth-first and breadth-first
OK   5.8 whatever-first search with a stack builds exactly RecursiveDFS's trees (all adjacency orders)  [401 graphs, every root]
OK   5.8 breadth-first tree depth = BFS distance (book's claim)
OK   5.8 a spanning tree that is both depth-first and breadth-first equals G  [501 such trees]
== Exercise 5.9: EagerWFS
OK   5.9(a) EagerWFS marks exactly the reachable vertices; parent edges form a spanning tree  [2000 graphs]
OK   5.9(b) EagerWFS with a queue == BFS: same marking order, same parents
OK   5.9(c) triangle: every EagerWFS tree (any bag, any order) is the star at s
OK   5.9(c) triangle: every RecursiveDFS tree is a path, never the star  [[[[0, 1], [1, 2]], [[0, 2], [1, 2]]]]
OK   5.9(c) triangle: with other start vertices RecursiveDFS can build the star at s (so both must start at s)
OK   5.9(c) K4, any start, any order, any bag: EagerWFS trees are the stars at the start, RecursiveDFS trees are Hamiltonian paths; none in common  [4 eager, 12 dfs trees]
== Exercise 5.10: three-color search
OK   5.10(a) no black vertex is ever adjacent to a white one  [1500 random runs]
OK   5.10(b) at the end: reachable = black, others white, parent edges = spanning tree
OK   5.10(b) at most 2V steps
OK   5.10(c) ThreeColorStackSearch runs == RecursiveDFS runs over all adjacency orders (order + parents)  [301 graphs]
OK   5.10(c) the stack always holds the gray vertices, each once, along a parent path
OK   5.10(d) K4: every BFS tree is the star; no ThreeColorQueue run (either push order) builds it
